{"name":"Angelic Choir","hex":"#e9d9dc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"angelic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Belonging to, or proceeding from, angels; resembling, characteristic of, or partaking of the nature of, an angel.","Very sweet-natured, well-behaved, or beautiful."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/angelic"},{"term":"choir","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A group of people who sing together; a company of people who are trained to sing together.","One quarter of a cruciform church, or the architectural area of a church, generally used by the choir; often near the apse."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sing in concert."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/choir"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hierarchy of angels","blocks":[{"text":"In the angelology of different religions, a hierarchy of angels is a ranking system of angels. The higher ranking angels have greater power and authority than lower ones, and different ranks have differences in appearance, such as varying numbers of wings or faces."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Nine_orders_of_angels.jpeg/330px-Nine_orders_of_angels.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierarchy_of_angels","wikidata":"Q6586528"},{"title":"Choir","blocks":[{"text":"A choir, also known as a chorale or chorus, is a musical ensemble of singers. Choral music, in turn, is the music written specifically for such an ensemble to perform or in other words is the music performed by the ensemble. Choirs may perform music from the classical music repertoire, which spans from the medieval era to the present, or popular music repertoire. Most choirs are led by a conductor, who leads the performances with arm, hand, and facial gestures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg/330px-Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choir","wikidata":"Q131186"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Florenca133b.jpg/500px-Florenca133b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Florenca133b.jpg/500px-Florenca133b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The ceiling mosaic of the Baptistery in Florence  (c. 1240–1300) depicts (in the innermost octagon of images) all nine of the orders of angelic beings:  the Seraphim and Cherubim are shown with Christ at lower center, while the other ranks each occupy a separate field, above which are their Latin designations.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlorenca133b.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/500px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/1280px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/500px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Assumption of the Virgin by Francesco Botticini (1475–77) at the National Gallery London, shows three hierarchies and nine orders of angels, each with different characteristics.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrancesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia